/* ==============================================================
   04 — MoveLin: the straight line
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   Linear motion, and why it is not the default
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, no tool mounted
   WATCH     Same start and end as example 03, different path
   TIME      ~30 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE COMMAND

       MoveLin(x, y, z, alpha, beta, gamma)

   Identical arguments to MovePose. Completely different promise.

   MovePose says: end up at this pose.
   MoveLin says:  end up at this pose, AND make the tool tip
                  travel there in a straight line, reorienting
                  smoothly as it goes.

   That guarantee is what you need whenever the path is part of
   the job:

     - pulling a part straight out of a fixture or a chuck
     - inserting a pin, a connector, a test probe
     - dispensing a bead of adhesive along an edge
     - approaching and retreating from a pick, so the gripper
       does not swing into the part it is about to grab

   WHAT IT COSTS

   A straight line in space is a complicated thing in joint
   space — the six joints have to be continuously coordinated to
   keep the tip on the line. Three consequences, all worth
   knowing before a customer finds them:

   1. IT IS SLOWER. Speed is bounded by the joint that is working
      hardest at the tightest point of the path. A joint move
      between the same two points is usually faster.

   2. IT CAN BE REFUSED. If the straight line would pass through
      a singularity or run a joint past its limit, the robot
      rejects the command and DOES NOT START MOVING. That is the
      right behaviour: you get an error, not a robot that stops
      halfway through an insertion. Note the difference from a
      joint move, which can almost always be executed.

   3. IT HOLDS THE ARM POSTURE. The start and end postures have
      to share the same configuration {cs, ce, cw} — the robot
      will not flip its elbow or wrist in the middle of a
      straight line. See 02_intermediate/04.

   THE RULE

   Use MoveLin only where the straight line is genuinely
   required — typically the last few centimetres into a part and
   the first few back out. Use MoveJoints or MovePose for
   everything else. This is the single most common mistake in a
   first robot program: making every move linear, then wondering
   why the cycle time is poor and why the robot occasionally
   refuses a position it can plainly reach.

   Note that the speed for these moves is set in mm/s with
   SetCartLinVel — a real velocity, not a percentage. See
   06_linear_motion_settings.mxprog.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(15)
SetCartLinVel(40)          // 40 mm/s. Slow enough to watch.

// --- Start from the zero position -----------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- The same three points as example 03 ----------------------
// Move into the starting pose. This first one is a MovePose,
// because getting into position is repositioning, not path work.
MovePose(190, 0, 248.9, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)

// Straight across in Y. Sight down the line and it holds.
MoveLin(190, 80, 248.9, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)

// This is the comparison that sells it. In 03_move_pose.mxprog
// the equivalent move bowed outward. Here the tip runs dead
// straight across and up, with the orientation blending evenly
// along the way.
MoveLin(190, -80, 308.9, 0, 90, 0)

Delay(1)


// --- The approach-and-retract pattern -------------------------
// This is the shape of nearly every real pick or place, and it
// is worth showing on its own.

// 1. Get above the target. Fast, path irrelevant, so MovePose.
MovePose(190, 0, 300, 0, 90, 0)

// 2. Descend straight down onto it. Path critical, so MoveLin.
MoveLin(190, 0, 260, 0, 90, 0)

// 3. (A gripper would close here. See 02_intermediate/07.)
Delay(0.5)

// 4. Lift straight back up before going anywhere. Skipping this
//    is how parts get dragged out of fixtures.
MoveLin(190, 0, 300, 0, 90, 0)


// --- Back to zero ---------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Run 03 and 04 back to back. Same endpoints, visibly
      different paths.

   2. Note that steps 2 and 4 of the approach pattern are really
      "go down 40 mm from here" and "go back up 40 mm". Writing
      absolute coordinates for that is fragile. There is a better
      way: MoveLinRelTrf, in 02_intermediate/03.

   NEXT   05_joint_motion_settings.mxprog — controlling speed.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
